INSERT INTO "messages" ("meta","seq","role","session_id","content","tool_call_id","inserted_at","id") VALUES ($1,$2,$3,$4,$5,$6,$7,$8) [%{"duration_ms" => 1, "ok" => true, "tool" => "read"}, 4, :tool, "bb749fdd-2e8e-4c43-900e-4984c7338aa3", "[benchapp/AGENTS.md: 653 lines, showing 1–653]\n1\tThis is a web application written using the Phoenix web framework.\n2\t\n3\t## Project guidelines\n4\t\n5\t- Use `mix precommit` alias when you are done with all changes and fix any pending issues\n6\t- Use the already included and available `:req` (`Req`) library for HTTP requests, **avoid** `:httpoison`, `:tesla`, and `:httpc`. Req is included by default and is the preferred HTTP client for Phoenix apps\n7\t\n8\t### Phoenix v1.8 guidelines\n9\t\n10\t- **Always** begin your LiveView templates with `<Layouts.app flash={@flash} ...>` which wraps all inner content\n11\t- The `MyAppWeb.Layouts` module is aliased in the `my_app_web.ex` file, so you can use it without needing to alias it again\n12\t- Anytime you run into errors with no `current_scope` assign:\n13\t  - You failed to follow the Authenticated Routes guidelines, or you failed to pass `current_scope` to `<Layouts.app>`\n14\t  - **Always** fix the `current_scope` error by moving your routes to the proper `live_session` and ensure you pass `current_scope` as needed\n15\t- Phoenix v1.8 moved the `<.flash_group>` component to the `Layouts` module. You are **forbidden** from calling `<.flash_group>` outside of the `layouts.ex` module\n16\t- For icons, use `<.icon name=\"hero-x-mark\" />` from `JobyKit.CoreComponents`. **Never** use `Heroicons` modules or similar — the kit's `<.icon>` component is the canonical entry point.\n17\t- **Always** use the imported `<.input>` from `JobyKit.CoreComponents` (registered in `design_manifest.ex` and surfaced on `/design`). Using it saves steps and prevents form-error mistakes.\n18\t- The kit's `<.input>` treats `class` as additive on top of the daisyUI defaults — pass utilities (margin, max-width, etc.) and the wrapper's identity classes (`input` / `select` / `textarea`) still apply.\n19\t\n20\t### JS and CSS guidelines\n21\t\n22\t- **Use Tailwind CSS classes and custom CSS rules** to create polished, responsive, and visually stunning interfaces.\n23\t- Tailwindcss v4 **no longer needs a tailwind.config.js** and uses a new import syntax in `app.css`:\n24\t\n25\t      @import \"tailwindcss\" source(none);\n26\t      @source \"../css\";\n27\t      @source \"../js\";\n28\t      @source \"../../lib/my_app_web\";\n29\t\n30\t- **Always use and maintain this import syntax** in the app.css file for projects generated with `phx.new`\n31\t- **Never** use `@apply` when writing raw css\n32\t- When a primitive UI need matches an existing core wrapper, use the wrapper instead of raw daisyUI or hand-rolled markup. JobyKit's goal is reuse and discoverability of existing app components, not generic daisyUI aesthetics. Page-level Tailwind for layout, motion, and visual hierarchy is encouraged; see \"Composition vs. creation\" below.\n33\t- Out of the box **only the app.js and app.css bundles are supported**\n34\t  - You cannot reference an external vendor'd script `src` or link `href` in the layouts\n35\t  - You must import the vendor deps into app.js and app.css to use them\n36\t  - **Never write inline <script>custom js</script> tags within templates**\n37\t\n38\t<!-- usage-rules-start -->\n39\t\n40\t<!-- phoenix:elixir-start -->\n41\t## Elixir guidelines\n42\t\n43\t- Elixir lists **do not support index based access via the access syntax**\n44\t\n45\t  **Never do this (invalid)**:\n46\t\n47\t      i = 0\n48\t      mylist = [\"blue\", \"green\"]\n49\t      mylist[i]\n50\t\n51\t  Instead, **always** use `Enum.at`, pattern matching, or `List` for index based list access, ie:\n52\t\n53\t      i = 0\n54\t      mylist = [\"blue\", \"green\"]\n55\t      Enum.at(mylist, i)\n56\t\n57\t- Elixir variables are immutable, but can be rebound, so for block expressions like `if`, `case`, `cond`, etc\n58\t  you *must* bind the result of the expression to a variable if you want to use it and you CANNOT rebind the result inside the expression, ie:\n59\t\n60\t      # INVALID: we are rebinding inside the `if` and the result never gets assigned\n61\t      if connected?(socket) do\n62\t        socket = assign(socket, :val, val)\n63\t      end\n64\t\n65\t      # VALID: we rebind the result of the `if` to a new variable\n66\t      socket =\n67\t        if connected?(socket" <> ..., "chatcmpl-tool-bc899ef5932fcaca", ~U[2026-09-11 08:30:42.489894Z], "8206edb9-fdf7-41fb-a53d-904711b73c64"]
