INSERT INTO "messages" ("meta","seq","role","session_id","content","tool_calls","reasoning","inserted_at","id") VALUES ($1,$2,$3,$4,$5,$6,$7,$8,$9) [%{}, 49, :assistant, "bb749fdd-2e8e-4c43-900e-4984c7338aa3", "\n\nNow update the manifest and previews for the new composites.\n\n", [%{"arguments" => %{"new_string" => "  # ----------------------------------------------------------------- composite\n  # Generic composites reused across pages live in\n  # `BenchappWeb.CompositeComponents` and are registered here so they\n  # surface on `/custom-designs` and in `/design.json`. Each carries a\n  # preview in `BenchappWeb.DesignPreviews`.\n\n  component CompositeComponents, :record_card,\n    category: :composite,\n    summary: \"A run record specimen: fields and units on a plotting-paper surface.\",\n    preview: &DesignPreviews.record_card_preview/1\n\n  component CompositeComponents, :spec_row,\n    category: :composite,\n    summary: \"Monospace label column beside a reading column; stacks on phone.\",\n    preview: &DesignPreviews.spec_row_preview/1", "old_string" => "  # ----------------------------------------------------------------- composite\n  # `empty_state` is the worked example — a real composite that bundles\n  # `<.icon>` + a heading + an optional action slot. Use it as the\n  # template for your own: copy the attr / slot / data-component shape,\n  # register the entry here, and add a preview in `design_previews.ex`.\n  #\n  # Generic composites belong in `BenchappWeb.CompositeComponents`;\n  # domain-specific ones in their own module (e.g.\n  # `BenchappWeb.ChatComponents`).\n\n  component CompositeComponents, :empty_state,\n    category: :composite,\n    summary: \"Centered icon + title + optional action; fills empty containers.\",\n    preview: &DesignPreviews.empty_state_preview/1\n\n  # -------------------------------------------------------------------- domain\n  # Add domain composites here:\n  #\n  #   component BenchappWeb.ChatComponents, :composer,\n  #     category: :domain,\n  #     summary: \"Message composer with response-length controls.\"", "path" => "/Users/jody/dgx-spark-model-bench/results/2026-09-10-baseline/work/qwen38-flash-next-nvfp4/benchapp/lib/benchapp_web/design_manifest.ex"}, "id" => "chatcmpl-tool-ae98a1c27b0b2ef6", "name" => "edit"}], "I removed empty_state. Now update design_manifest.ex to register the two composites (drop empty_state), and design_previews.ex to add previews for them and drop empty_state_preview.\n\nI need to import CompositeComponents so I can call `<.record_card>` in LiveViews. Currently `import JobyKit.CoreComponents` is in html_helpers, but CompositeComponents is not imported. In home_live.ex they used `<.card>` (core) directly. To use `<.record_card>` in LiveViews I need to either import CompositeComponents or alias + full call. Let me add `import BenchappWeb.CompositeComponents` to html_helpers in benchapp_web.ex so `<.record_card>` and `<.spec_row>` resolve everywhere. That's clean and app-wide.\n\nWait — would importing CompositeComponents into html_helpers cause the lint to consider record_card/spec_row as needing registration? They ARE registered. Good. Also the `use BenchappWeb, :html` in composite_components itself imports CoreComponents; fine.\n\nBut careful: adding `import BenchappWeb.CompositeComponents` to html_helpers — but CompositeComponents itself does `use BenchappWeb, :html`, which imports CompositeComponents into itself → self import. Is that circular/problematic? A module importing itself is fine in Elixir as long as it's compiled. But there's a compile-order concern: html_helpers imports BenchappWeb.CompositeComponents; CompositeComponents uses BenchappWeb :html which expands the same import referencing itself. Self-referential import within the same module is allowed. However, at compile time, when compiling CompositeComponents, the quote imports `BenchappWeb.CompositeComponents` which is the module being compiled — Elixir will warn \"you are importing the module you're defining\" or it may work. This could be a problem (cyclic). \n\nSafer approach: don't add the import app-wide. Instead, in each LiveView, `alias BenchappWeb.CompositeComponents` and call `<CompositeComponents.record_card .../>`. But brief says use wrappers with `<.name>` form ideally. The `<CompositeComponents.record_card>` form is fully valid and used elsewhere (`JobyKit.CoreComponents.theme_toggle` in layouts, `JobyKit.PageComponent...` in custom_designs). So that's fine and avoids the circular import.\n\nActually the cleanest: add a helper import in the html_helpers quoting `import BenchappWeb.CompositeComponents`, but composite_components.ex uses `use BenchappWeb, :html`. To avoid the self-import cycle, I can make composite_components.ex NOT use the app-wide import of itself. But it does `use BenchappWeb, :html`.\n\nLet me check: does importing a module into itself cause an error? In Elixir, `import SomeModule` from within `SomeModule` typically produces a compile warning \"cannot import into itself\" or it silently does nothing? Let me recall... Actually Elixir raises `CompileError: cannot import BenchappWeb.CompositeComponents from itself`? Hmm, I think `use`/`import` of self can error. There's `Module.put_attribute` ... I recall the error \"you have already required X\" ... To be safe, I'll NOT do app-wide import. Instead I'll use the namespaced call form in LiveViews. That's guaranteed to compile and matches kit style.\n\nSo: in HomeLive/ResearchLive/AboutLive I'll add `alias BenchappWeb.CompositeComponents` and call `<CompositeComponents.record_card ...>`.\n\nFor DesignPreviews and DesignManifest they already alias CompositeComponents. Good.\n\nNow update the manifest.\n", ~U[2026-09-11 08:36:41.567832Z], "e6ab5422-f96d-4c29-822b-b9a69b8499a0"]
